{"id": "bdad4a96-9308-4aea-aaae-c53b0c009cab", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T20:08:49.130399", "source": "user", "parent_id": "c7538efa-193c-497e-a1d9-a44ccc90afb1", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{"researcher_request": "Approach approved — proceed to the full experiment design. The reframe is the right call: estimate a direction effect net of target notability and answer-set cardinality, and treat \"survives only in confounded subsets\" as a legitimate negative verdict. Environment findings match the brief (design-only, no dataset/harness, no network), so specifying execution preconditions rather than running anything is correct.\n\nWrite the full design (rationale, stimuli, probes, controls, metrics, analysis plan, feasibility). Four things I want made explicit, since they are where the design will be judged:\n\n1. Make the pre-registration operational, not nominal. Give the actual decision rule as a testable statement: the primary estimand, the pre-specified minimum effect size (state the metric it is expressed on, e.g. an odds ratio or a percentage-point accuracy gap on the only-child subset), the CI rule, and what fraction of items the four survive-subsets (only-child, notability-reversed, surname-mismatch, mention-balanced) must retain to count as a test rather than a shrunken residual. If a subset falls below usable N, say in advance which conclusion is forfeited.\n\n2. Specify the notability-reversed sampling procedure concretely, since it is load-bearing: the notability index (which of pageviews / sitelinks / in-corpus frequency enters it and in what combination), the reversal threshold, and how you avoid selecting a degenerate stratum — e.g. reverse-famous children who are famous *because* of the parent, which re-collapses direction into notability. Give the expected yield of such families from a Wikidata-derived pool as a projected, not measured, figure. If the yield is small, say so and state the power consequence.\n\n3. Pin the mention-symmetry control to something reproducible. \"Mention counts in both articles\" needs a defined extractor (infobox children field vs. prose detection of son/daughter/child-of constructions, which revision or section, which snapshot date), otherwise the mention-balanced subset is not replicable. Also state whether you condition on mention count as a covariate, restrict to a balanced subset, or both — conditioning and subsetting answer slightly different questions, so say which carries the primary weight.\n\n4. Report effect sizes and CIs in addition to the mixed model, and pre-commit to reporting per-template gaps alongside pooled results, so the design cannot hide paraphrase variance behind a pooled term.\n\nScope guards, so this stays one deliverable: the human baseline (with/without search) and the RAG upper bound are valuable but secondary — include them as clearly scoped optional arms with their purpose stated, not as co-primary. Keep the capital-of / spouse-of generic-inversion control in the core, since it changes the interpretation of the headline claim. The CPU-only pilot can be a one-paragraph feasibility note. State the power analysis as assumption- or simulation-based, given no pilot data exists here, and do not attach a fabricated observed effect size to it.\n\nDo not present any figure, item count, or model result as measured; every quantity that depends on execution should be labeled as an assumed, projected, or to-be-estimated value.", "required_handoff_stage": "result", "approach_approved": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "0e534c07-bf7e-4898-873e-14f954bb5648", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T20:13:16.404974", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "bdad4a96-9308-4aea-aaae-c53b0c009cab", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Approve noted — writing the full design now with the four judgement points made operational."}], "reasoning_content": "Now I need to write the full experiment design, with the four explicit items. Let me be careful, thorough, and label all quantities as assumed/projected/to-be-estimated.

Let me think about the concrete details so the design is defensible.

### Notability index

Define a composite notability index from three sources, all retrievable reproducibly:
1. **Pageviews**: English Wikipedia pageviews for the article, summed over a fixed 12-month window that ends at the item snapshot date (e.g., 2025-01-01 to 2025-12-31), log10(1+mean daily views). Source: Wikimedia REST `pageviews/per-article`.
2. **Sitelinks**: count of Wikidata sitelinks (all language Wikipedias) as of snapshot — a cross-lingual notability proxy, robust to pageview noise and bot traffic.
3. **In-corpus name frequency**: log10 frequency of the exact full name (and "LAST, First" variant) in a fixed English Wikipedia text snapshot (the dump matching the snapshot date), with article-level dedup (count of distinct articles mentioning the name, plus total occurrences). This is the closest available proxy to training-data exposure.

Composite: z-score each within (era of fame, occupation domain) strata, then average with weights. I should give weights explicitly and pre-register them: primary index = mean of z(pageviews), z(sitelinks), z(article-mention-count) — equal weights; and pre-register two sensitivity indices (pageviews-only; article-mention-count-only) to show robustness to index construction. Also pre-register using pagerank-ish? Keep three, it's enough. Add: exclude "famous because of parent" via a separate criterion (see below).

**Reversal threshold**: Define ΔN = N(child) − N(parent). Require ΔN ≥ 1.0 in composite z units AND ratio of pageviews ≥ 5× AND ratio of article-mention counts ≥ 3× (conjunctive, so reversal is not index-construction artifact). Sensitivity threshold at ΔN ≥ 1.5.

**Avoiding the degenerate "famous because of parent" stratum.** Key operationalization — a set of independent notability-source criteria for the child:
(a) The child must have their own English Wikipedia article whose lead-section P106 (occupation) is *not* solely derived-from-parent categories — concretely: child must have at least one **independent notability marker**: an award/credit-earning claim in Wikidata that does not reference the parent (e.g., P166 award received, P106 occupation with an own-body-of-work), and/or an article of length ≥ some threshold with ≥ N independent references.
(b) **Parent-mention-rate ceiling in the child's own article**: compute the fraction of the child's article sentences that mention the parent. If the child's coverage is overwhelmingly parent-referential (e.g., >X% of mentions of the family in the child article are the parent, and the parent appears in the lead), flag as parent-derived notability and exclude from the reversed stratum. Better and more principled: **counterfactual notability residual** — regress log pageviews on (i) whether the person appears in the parent's article, (ii) article length, (iii) years since article creation, and (iv) *the parent's* notability; then require the child to have a high positive residual. That's clean: a child who is famous *because of* the parent should be predicted well by parent notability + co-mention; a genuinely independently-famous child has a large positive residual. Use a simple pre-registered model: `logPV_child ~ logPV_parent + logPV_max_sibling + article_len_child + n_sitelinks_parent + age_child_article`, fit on the full pool; reversed stratum requires predicted value below actual by ≥ 1 log unit. I like this. But it must be specified as an OLS on log-counts with pre-specified terms — reproducible.
(c) **Temporal ordering criterion**: the child's article creation date must precede or be independent of the parent's peak-notability window? Too convoluted. Use: the child's notability must have a rising trend before age 25 or something — skip.
(d) Domain criterion: prefer children whose notability derives from an occupation domain *different* from the parent's primary P106 domain (e.g., parent actor, child scientist/athlete/politician). Cross-domain pairs are strong evidence of independent notability. Use as a required-or criterion: at least one of (independent-notability-marker) or (cross-domain occupation) must hold; parent-mention-rate ceiling must hold in all cases.

